    bio: "Amanirenas was a Kandake — a queen regnant — of the Kingdom of Kush, the Nubian civilisation centred at Meroë in what is now Sudan. She ruled at one of the most dramatic moments in her kingdom\'s history, and she is remembered above all for something almost no ruler of her era managed: she fought the Roman Empire and forced it to negotiate on terms favourable to her own people.\n\nWhen the Roman Emperor Augustus annexed Egypt in 30 BC following the defeat of Mark Antony and Cleopatra, the new Roman province shared its southern border with Kush. The relationship deteriorated rapidly. Around 25 BC, taking advantage of a Roman military expedition to Arabia that had left the Egyptian frontier thinly defended, Amanirenas led a Kushite army north into Roman territory, sacking the frontier cities of Aswan, Elephantine, and Philae. Her forces tore down statues of Augustus and carried the severed bronze head of one back to Meroë, where it was buried beneath the steps of a temple — so that ever after, Romans entering the sacred space would unknowingly tread on the face of their emperor.\n\nThe Roman response was swift and punishing. The general Gaius Petronius drove the Kushites back, sacked the Nubian city of Napata, and took thousands of prisoners. Amanirenas — described by the Greek geographer Strabo as a 'masculine' and 'courageous' woman, blind in one eye, possibly from a wound sustained during the war — refused to surrender or cease resistance. She continued fighting and harassing Roman positions for years, forcing Petronius into a grinding and expensive campaign that neither side could decisively win.\n\nThe eventual peace treaty, signed around 21 or 20 BC, was remarkably favourable to Kush. Augustus agreed to return the prisoners taken by Petronius, waived the tribute Rome had originally demanded, and fixed the frontier at a point that gave Kush effective sovereignty over a significant buffer zone. It was one of the very few occasions in the Augustan era when a foreign power negotiated with Rome as something close to an equal — and it was achieved by a one-eyed African queen who had been written off as a barbarian insurgent. Amanirenas left no autobiography and built no monument that survives with her name clearly attached, but the bronze head of Augustus, recovered by archaeologists in 1910, still exists. It is one of the finest Roman portrait sculptures in existence — and the story of how it got to Sudan is entirely hers."
